{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My strength.</b> R. Solomon [Rashi] of blessed memory said that there is a difference between עָזִי with kamatz ḥatef and עֻזִי with qibbuts; therefore he said that the yod of עזי וזמרת יה is superfluous, but he showed us none like it. And he said that וזמרת יה is in construct, as if written עֹז וזמרת יה היה לי לישועה, for he thought that its parallel is ויהי ביום השלישי וישא אברהם את עיניו (Genesis 22:4). But whoever understands the language of Ishmael will know the difference between them, for to say עזי וזמרת יה ויהי לי לישועה is neither the grammar of the holy language nor of the Ishmaelite language. And there is no difference between עָזִי with kamatz ḗatef and its being with qibbuts, for he said הֲטִרְפֵנִי חֻקִּי (Proverbs 30:5)—it is said from it: כִּי חָקְךָ וְחֹק בָּנֶיךָ (Leviticus 10:13). And behold, in this song: נְהַלְתָּ בְעָזְךָ (Exodus 15:13) with kamatz ḥatef, and like it: YHWH בְעָזְךָ יִשְׂמַח מֶלֶךְ (Psalms 21:2). And behold, the yod is a sign of the speaker, said R. Moses the Kohen, because of the resh of וְזַמְרָת with qamatz, for it is וְזַמְרָתִי יָהּ: אִם אֶתֵּן שֵׁנַת לְעֵינָי (Psalms 132:4) with qamatz, like שְׁנָתִי. And behold: נַחֲלַת שְׁפָרִי עָלַי (Psalms 16:6) with qamatz like נַחֲלָתִי (Ruth 4:6); YHWH מְנָת חֶלְקִי וְכוֹסִי (Psalms 16:5) with qamatz, like מְנָתִי. And behold, what shall one do with the word וּמְנָת הַמֶּלֶךְ (2 Chronicles 31:3), which is qamatz and is in construct—and thus יָם הַמֶּלַח (Numbers 34:3), יָם כִּנֶּרֶת (Numbers 34:11)? And whoever answers that these are irregularities, it shall be said to him: place with them עָזִי וְזַמְרָת יָהּ. And in my opinion, the word עזי draws itself and another with it, according to the grammar of the holy language, as though written: עזי וזמרת עזי יה. And the reason: because above he mentioned סוֹס וְרֹכְבוֹ רָמָה בַיָּם, he acknowledged that this was not by his own pride and strength, but עזי ותפארת עזי is HaShem alone, who is יה. And this is one of three proper nouns which I have explained, all three from one root. And because the word אהיה is known in the language, it was necessary to substitute the vav for the yod in the honorable Name, and the word יה is known, and on account of the guttural letter the yod was opened, and the hidden yod they removed, for it has another sod by way of number. And we find the Name joined with the honorable Name, and it is like עזי וזמרת יה YHWH. And I have already explained that HaShem is sometimes a proper noun and sometimes an adjective:" "<b>This is my El.</b> My might:" "<b>And I will adorn Him.</b> I will establish Him in a habitation:" "And this serves on account of another: זֶה אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי, and the meaning is the El of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob:" "<b>And I will exalt Him.</b> That I will recount His might:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Read בקמץ חטף throughout as kamatz ḥatef; the commentator's argument turns on this vocalization distinction against Rashi.", 1 "Ibn Ezra glosses זה אלי with תקיפי ('my strength/might'), apparently interpreting it not as demonstration but as apposition.", 2 "No note.", 3 "No note.", 4 "No note."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:55.806014910Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}